WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.380
[intro music]

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.680
With no further ado

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.320
give it up for Neil!

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:15.980
[applause]

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.620
Good morning John, it's Tuesday May 1st

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:23.920
That was New York Times Bestselling Author, Hugo Award Winner, Nebula Award Winner, and all around

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.620
awesome nerdfighter guy Neil Gaiman!

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.180
Now I know that you are a writer and that you hang out with writers and that you always have good writers

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.460
on Brotherhood 2.0

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.020
But now, I've got one!

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.500
I may have had to wait in a very, very long line

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.060
to get one

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:41.260
but I got one

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.580
and he's saying hi

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:47.020
Congratulations to Coe! LA Times book award, that's a big deal!

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.120
Congratulations to you, John, for getting nominated!

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.220
And since I know a lot of the nerdfighters are Neil Gaiman fans,

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.560
and I know a lot of the nerdfighters want to someday be writers,

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.360
I think I'm gonna share with you a clip from Neil's lecture that I surreptitiously captured

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.080
Someone asked him if there was really anything better

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.940
than seeing your name on the spine of a book

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:07.220
And having watched you go through this, John, I can attest that he is right.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:11.340
I'm actually pretty sure that you fell victim to Gaiman's Law, which says that

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.580
as soon as your first book is published, you will go to the bookstore

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.440
and open the book to a random page, on which you will find

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.100
possibly the only

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.640
typographical error in your entire book

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.500
and then you will want to kill yourself

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.380
so here's Neil talking about being an author

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:25.820
It's not another day at the office.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.140
It's magic

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:30.500
It's, You know, I can't think of - there's nothing else I would want to do.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.540
Um, it's every bit as good as you'd think it would be

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.820
But, it's like one of those things, that when you're

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:43.480
a kid, you think, "If I just did that once, who would ever need anything more, ever?"

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:47.180
It's like when you're fourteen and you finally figure out what sex is

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.320
[laughter]

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.660
and then you cannot for the life of you understand why adults do anything else.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.780
[laughter]

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.180
So there you have it!

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.300
Thanks for challenging me to write more songs

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.940
As a commenter pointed out, it is, actually kind of

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:06.280
difficult and requires lots of creative energy, so

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.840
I can't guarantee anything

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.860
Uh, sometimes you just draw a blank, right?

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:14.300
So there were other reasons besides Neil Gaiman that I was in Helena, Montana

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.280
[Hank, singing ominously in the background]:  ♫ Helena Montana ♫

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:19.440
That was weird

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:20.360
did you-

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.260
[singing in the background]: ♫ Helana Montana ♫

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:23.200
Whoa, oh!

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.680
Oh - I - Okay, I think it's time for a song!

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.740
♫ Helena Montana, Helena Montanta ♫

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:34.800
Helena, Montana. Helena Montana

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.480
Gold and cows and chopping trees, these are the industries

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.720
of Helena, Montana

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.480
They serve your eggs with butter and there are peeps in the gutter

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.880
Oh, Helena, Montana

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.220
Helena, Montana

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:53.980
We played Frisbee at the capitol and looked at lots of belt buckles. Helena, Montana.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:59.400
Check your guns at the door, fat guy at a candy store, Helena, Montana.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.280
Helena, Montana.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.860
Well I don't know if that was anything like as catchy as the Helen Hunt song but maybe it'll

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:08.440
bump it out of some of you guys' heads

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.500
Still no progress on that "me not being paid for my work" front

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.680
And I'm very excited that Coe Booth thinks I'm hot

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:15.880
and I'd like to say hi to Francesca Lia Block

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.740
And to all of the other amazing people who said hi to me in the last videoblog.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.320
Hello to you all!

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.680
We may not be famous, but we got famous people on the Brotherhood!

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.100
I'll see you tommorrow!

